<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#afrikaans">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afrikaans</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afrikaans is a West Germanic language that developed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> spoken by settlers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, with influence from Malay, Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe</a> and other languages. It was recognised as an official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in place of Dutch in 1925. Today it is one of South Africa's official languages and is also widely spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afrikaans.jpg" alt="Afrikaans" width="285" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fobos92 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idioma_afrik%C3%A1ans.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Afrikaans is a West Germanic language that emerged during the late 19th century as a fusion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> dialects, mainly Hollandic and Zeelandic, spoken by Dutch settlers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> since the 17th century, and the Dutch-based creole languages spoken by the Khoisan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> and Coloured peoples. Its name comes from the Dutch word Afrikaansch, meaning African. It was previously called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-dutch">Cape Dutch</a>, or the derogatory kitchen Dutch, from its use by slaves of colonial settlers in the kitchen.</p><p>For a long time the Eastern Border Hypothesis of the linguist Christo van Rensburg was regarded as the scientific consensus. More recent research concludes that the development was more gradual and diverse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> had a highly diverse linguistic landscape until the early twentieth century, from communities where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> differed little from Dutch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to communities for whom Dutch was a second or third language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>An estimated 90 to 95 per cent of the vocabulary is of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> origin, although Afrikaans has also adopted words from German, Malay and Khoisan languages. It differs from Dutch in its more analytic morphology and grammar and in some spellings, and the two languages are largely mutually intelligible, especially in writing. Many consonants of the earlier Dutch spelling are dropped: Dutch slechts becomes slegs, and zuid becomes suid. A particular feature is its double negative, which is absent from the other West Germanic standard languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Afrikaans is an official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and a recognised national language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>. Before independence in 1990 it had equal status with German as an official language in Namibia. Contrary to popular belief, the majority of its speakers today are Coloured people, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> it shares its status with ten other official languages and has lost the preferential treatment of earlier times in education and media. Even so, it remains more prevalent in radio, newspapers and television than any other official language except English, and more than 300 book titles are published in it annually.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Afrikaans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Afrikaans</b> grew out of the Cape Dutch vernacular, and the Afrikaner nationalist movement had early roots in the colony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…who founded the National Party in 1914, governed from 1924 in a pact with the Labour Party, made <b>Afrikaans</b> an official language in 1925 and won recognition of dominion equality in the Balfour Declaration of 1926, confirmed by the Statute of Westminster in 1931 and the Status of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Through early contact with the Philippines, words such as dalam hati, “sympathy”, luwalhati, “glory”, tengah hari, “midday”, and sedap, “delicious”, entered Tagalog and other Philippine languages, and the early settlement of a Cape Malay community in Cape Town brought many Classical Malay words into <b>Afrikaans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance revived in the 1970s: Black Consciousness inspired a new generation, strikes in Durban in 1973 brought out tens of thousands of workers, and on 16 June 1976 police in Soweto fired on schoolchildren marching against the use of <b>Afrikaans</b> in their schools, killing Hector Pieterson among others.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The movement heavily supported the protests that led to the Soweto uprising of 16 June 1976, which began when black students were required to learn <b>Afrikaans</b> and many secondary classes were to be taught in it.</blockquote><a href="/movement/black-consciousness-movement">Black Consciousness Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In May 1918 C. J. Langenhoven wrote an <b>Afrikaans</b> poem called &quot;Die Stem&quot; (&quot;The Voice&quot;).</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-stem-van-suid-afrika">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</a></li></ul></section>
